WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.040
I am in the National Advanced Driving Simulator
at the University of Iowa.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.960
It's used to test drivers' reactions
in a controlled, repeatable environment

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.660
and also to test things that would
be unsafe in the real world.

00:00:11.665 --> 00:00:13.990
- Tom's currently in the
simulator, undergoing a study

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.914
to look at driver response
to unintended acceleration.

00:00:16.914 --> 00:00:19.343
The vehicle is going
to accelerate quickly

00:00:19.343 --> 00:00:23.160
without any input from Tom,
as if it were malfunctioning.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.440
Tom's going to have to identify that this event is going on

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.500
and press the brake very quickly
to bring the car under control.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.120
GPS: Turn left at the next intersection.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.680
Whoa...

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.380
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.640
That was terrifying!

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.180
But there's a question
I want to answer here.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.200
Recently, I did a video while driving.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.252
And it was on a dead straight road
with very little traffic,

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:55.181
and I always felt in control of the vehicle. 
But was I?

00:00:55.181 --> 00:00:58.740
Is it actually dangerous to talk
into a camera while driving?

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.880
So, I'm going to spend 15 minutes
in this simulator, distracted,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.540
trying to give this monologue
to that camera.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.200
And I'm also going to spend
15 minutes in here, not distracted

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.300
just driving, and putting my
full attention on the road,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.740
and the team here
are going to analyse my results

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.580
and tell me how I've done.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.140
And given I was just doing 35 in a 25,
probably not that great.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.500
- The National Advanced Driving Simulator
was built to recreate

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.380
the most realistic
driving experience possible anywhere.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.800
It has a real vehicle cab in it.

00:01:29.808 --> 00:01:32.475
We can swap different
vehicle cabs, in and out.

00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:36.938
The motion base can travel 64 feet
in the X and Y directions

00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:39.807
and these are basically steel
belts that are connected to

00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:42.929
large drives on either end and then,

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:45.775
the hexapod portion of the
simulator is hydraulic,

00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:49.167
that allows us to experience
roll, pitch and yaw.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:53.120
We also have a turntable that can
turn 330 degrees in either direction.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.480
Overall, it has 13 independent
degrees of freedom.

00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:01.239
We have absolute control
of what time of day it is.

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:04.139
We have control over
the weather and traffic.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.460
- And yes, the graphics might not be up to
modern video game standards,

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.020
but after a few minutes in here,
with all the motion,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.620
your brain just accepts that you're driving.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.800
- Very broadly, we are
interested in studying the relation

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.420
between the driver and the vehicle
to improve safety,

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.460
so that encompasses things like
driver impairment, driver drowsiness,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.360
distraction or intoxication.
So we bring drivers in,

00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:26.150
actually give them a
large dose of alcohol,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.027
get them in the simulator and
we can see how they perform.

00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:31.000
We've also compared that
to things like cannabis,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.720
so some of the first studies
with cannabis and driving

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.680
have been done in
the simulator here as well.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.780
As Tom is driving in the simulator,
talking to the video camera,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.060
I would expect to see him missing
certain information in the driving scene.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.260
Generally, we find that
interactions that take the

00:02:44.266 --> 00:02:47.129
driver's eyes off the road
tend to be the worst but,

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:48.943
even in situations where the
driver's looking straight ahead,

00:02:48.943 --> 00:02:53.800
if their attention is pulled to another task,
like talking to a video camera,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.200
that can also increase distraction and cause
the driver to miss certain things.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.454
If the driver crashes, they
essentially move through

00:03:01.454 --> 00:03:03.626
the vehicle or the pedestrian
that they collided with,

00:03:03.626 --> 00:03:05.719
so, we don't simulate the
dynamics of the crash,

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:07.982
but we are recording that they crashed.

00:03:07.982 --> 00:03:09.582
We know the velocity of that crash.

00:03:09.582 --> 00:03:12.695
We know how severe that
crash would have been.

00:03:12.695 --> 00:03:14.286
- So, how did I do?

00:03:14.286 --> 00:03:16.787
- About as well as we would have predicted.

00:03:16.787 --> 00:03:19.435
Let me pull up an example, here.

00:03:19.435 --> 00:03:21.429
A vehicle is going to drive
through an intersection.

00:03:21.429 --> 00:03:23.428
You actually have the red stop sign.

00:03:23.428 --> 00:03:24.828
- Yeah, I didn't see the stop sign, did I?

00:03:24.828 --> 00:03:26.193
- Completely missed it.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.540
- Whoa, just did not see that.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.136
The thing is, I'm actually
looking at the road, there.

00:03:32.136 --> 00:03:34.864
I'm actually... but I'm not
thinking about the road,

00:03:34.864 --> 00:03:37.850
because I'm too busy thinking
about what my next line is.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.163
- Exactly, we would call this
inattentional blindness.

00:03:40.163 --> 00:03:41.369
This is when your
attention is off the road,

00:03:41.369 --> 00:03:42.906
even though your eyes are there.

00:03:42.906 --> 00:03:45.484
You're missing information
that's out in the environment.

00:03:45.484 --> 00:03:46.411
- Wow.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.860
- So, we saw quite a bit of
swerving at the beginning of your drive,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.159
you had some trouble
controlling your speed.

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:56.253
You were going about 35
through a 25 school zone.

00:03:56.253 --> 00:03:58.386
Textbook case of distracted driving.

00:03:58.386 --> 00:04:01.932
- So, I basically, shouldn't
film while driving.

00:04:01.932 --> 00:04:03.748
- Generally speaking, yes.

00:04:03.748 --> 00:04:05.895
- Thank you so much.
- Thank you.

00:04:05.895 --> 00:04:07.004
- Thank you so much to everyone at the

00:04:07.004 --> 00:04:10.020
National Advanced Driving Simulator
at the University of Iowa.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.860
Pull down the description or click
the link on screen for more about them.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.840
All the pedestrians here are just suicidal.
All of them.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.018
This is not a stop sign.
I can just take this.

00:04:20.018 --> 00:04:23.237
Whoa, no I can't.
You're coming in from the right.

00:04:23.237 --> 00:04:26.282
Probably shouldn't check
my phone while I'm driving.

